#{[17592186056138 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10 \\\\ 8 & 12 & 14\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056139 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 12 & 15 \\\\ 1 & 24 & 28\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056140 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 7\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056141 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 6 & 7\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056142 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 7\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056150 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056151 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056152 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -9 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056153 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056154 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -9 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056215 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}-1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056216 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056217 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056218 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056219 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056227 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]$,\n$x = 1$ and $y =-1$"] [17592186056228 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]$,\n$x = 1$ and $y =-1$"] [17592186056229 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]$,\n$x = 7$ and $y =-1$"] [17592186056230 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]$,\n$x = 7$ and $y =-1$"] [17592186056231 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]$,\n$x =-3$ and $y = 1$"] [17592186058999 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186059000 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186059001 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186059002 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186059003 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186059579 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $a,b,c,d$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n![](images/93/93874fa93cfd9ef3df1492bbc35056c2381157740d8ca26b475474faf54ee4d1.png)\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n![](images/83/8356d9148bc06d76a5aee41e4a07651c43d94b2496d9542eb8b6e01ee52ea161.png)"] [17592186059580 :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 2+(-4)(2) & -1+(-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059581 :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059582 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nNow, applying the operation $R_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4+1\\cdot (-4) & 1+1\\cdot (-2) & 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation \n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1.\n$$"] [17592186059583 :question/worked-solution "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0.$\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0,$ because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0.$\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1.$ If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22},$ we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1.\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1+(-5)\\cdot 3 & 5+(-5)\\cdot 1 & 6+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1,\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 -5 R_1.\n$$"] [17592186059584 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n![](images/ba/baf05a693deacc3ef644165bae4de7b1435a90c73f050ac76c48892b2e010393.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=1$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2(1)=0 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-2$ and $y=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left<  -2,1 \\right>.$"] [17592186059585 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/87/8773defc4db521e846b58b7c5ef04b54f26723d94099c75caffbeca0587c33dc.svg)\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{array}] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n![](images/45/455dc49a531a371ea8aecd6259a677c8006bb2a23181ed66d27721135f240c89.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-10$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-5(-10)=-5 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-55.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-55$ and $y=-10.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -55,-10 \\rangle.$"] [17592186059956 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ on $M?$"] [17592186059956 :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5+(-1)(5) & -1+(-1)(2) & 2+(-1)(-1) & 1+(-1)(1) \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059961 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}$ on $M?$"] [17592186059961 :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $3$ times the first row to the third row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3+(3)(-1) & -1+(3)(2) & 9+(3)(4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 21\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059962 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$"] [17592186059962 :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059963 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A$, given by\n$A = \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 7\\end{array}\\right]$.\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_{1}:=2R_{1}$ on $A$?"] [17592186059965 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -9 & -21 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right]$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form."] [17592186059969 :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\\begin{cases}6x+y+z=4 \\\\ x+z=9\\end{cases}$$"] [17592186059969 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\\begin{cases}6x+y+z=4 \\\\ x+0y+z=9\\end{cases}$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}6 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [17592186059970 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases} & x-3y=-8 \\\\  & 2x+4y=4.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186059970 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\\begin{cases} & x-3y=-8 \\\\  & y=2,\\end{cases}$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=2$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-3(2)=-8\\,⇒\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-2$ and $y=2.$"] [17592186059972 :question/problem "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$\\begin{cases}-2x + 4y = 20 \\\\ - x =-13\\end{cases}$"] [17592186059976 :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$\\begin{cases}\\frac{c}{3} =-a \\\\ - 4 + 2b = 2c + a \\\\ d = 3\\end{cases}$"] [17592186059978 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9 & 5\\end{array}\\right]$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix."] [17592186060198 :question/problem "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n$\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{array}\\right]$"] [17592186060201 :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of  linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{cases}-x+y=5 \\\\ x+y=6\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060201 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the system is already organized so that the variables are in the order $x,y$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient. \n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables: \n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 6\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [17592186060202 :question/problem "Given that\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{22}=0?$"] [17592186060202 :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}.\n$$"] [17592186060205 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases} & 2x+7y=-5 \\\\  & 7x+11y=-4.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186060205 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{27}{2} & \\frac{27}{2}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{27}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060394 :question/problem "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{cases}-x+y=2 \\\\ x-y=-2\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060394 :question/worked-solution "The coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186060395 :question/problem "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [17592186060395 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix consists of two parts: the coefficient matrix and the column of constants.\n\nThe coefficient matrix represents the coefficients in the linear system, while the column of constants represents the constants on the right-hand sides of the equations.\n\nUsing the variables $x$ and  $y$,  the corresponding system of equations is\n\n$$\\begin{cases}x-2y=10 \\\\ -x+y=-2.\\end{cases}$$"] [17592186060396 :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$\\begin{cases}2a - b =-1 \\\\ a + 2b = 3\\end{cases}$"] [17592186060938 :tutorial/content "We can use matrices to solve systems of linear equations. To do this, we need to set up the **augmented matrix** for the system.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}2}x+{\\color{red}5}y={\\color{blue}-1} \\\\ {\\color{red}-3}x+{\\color{red}7}y={\\color{blue}1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the **coefficient matrix**, which consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{matrix}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}5} \\\\ {\\color{red}-3} & {\\color{red}7}\\end{matrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we create the **augmented matrix**. This is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}5} & {\\color{blue}-1} \\\\ {\\color{red}-3} & {\\color{red}7} & {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nIn the future, we will learn how to use coefficient and augmented matrices to solve systems of equations. But for now, let's practice setting them up."] [17592186060940 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can apply some operations to the augmented matrix that do not change the set of solutions for the corresponding system. These operations are called **elementary row operations**. \n\nAfter applying an **elementary row operation** to the augmented matrix, the new corresponding system will have the same solutions.\n\nThere are three types of elementary row operations:\n\n- switching any two rows: \n\n$$\nR_i \\leftrightarrow R_j\n$$\n\n- multiplying (each element of) a row by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nR_i := k R_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- adding a multiple of one row to another row: \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j+k R_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\nIn the notations above, the \"$:=$\" symbol is called the **assignment operator.** We use this to assign new values to every element of a given row.\n\nFor example, the row operation $R_1 := 2 R_1$ means, \"create a new row $1$ by taking the old row $1$ and multiplying it by $2.$\" When we do this, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}]\\,{R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA few final points on notation.\n\n- We use the similarity symbol ($\\sim$) to indicate when one matrix is transformed into another matrix using elementary row operations.\n\n- The assignment operator $:=$ is sometimes called the \"walrus operator\" because it looks like a walrus on its side!"] [17592186060941 :tutorial/content "Consider the following system of linear equations and its respective **augmented matrix**:\n![](images/2c/2c5dfe046037750f98f773ad906094f5ffbad3c285adf200134a119f72888ac8.svg)\n\nIf we can convert the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form, then we can solve the system using back substitution.\n\nThe procedure for reducing $M$ to row echelon form using elementary row operations is called **Gaussian elimination**. To perform Gaussian elimination on a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we use the following procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Consider the entry $a_{11}=2$ of the coefficient matrix. This entry is called a **pivot element**. We want to make this entry equal to $1$. So, the first elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{array}] & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Consider the entry $a_{21}=3$ below the first pivot element. We want to make this entry equal to $0$. So, the second elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=R_2+(-3)R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+({\\color{blue}-3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & -10 & 5\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, consider the entry $a_{22}=-10$. This entry is the second pivot element, and we would like to make it equal to $1$. So, the third elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=\\dfrac{1}{-10}R_2\n$$\n\nand we get $M$ in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{red}-10} & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{{\\color{red}-10}}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{red}1} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n![](images/b8/b83c4646349bada803311e5e9e160aafc8f08e6f51c7b69302def5ca4eeaa3fa.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3 \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = -1 \n\t\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\tx=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left< \\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right>.$"] [17592186129771 :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 3 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 5\\end{array}\\right],\\,B=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}3 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right],\\,C=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186129771 :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 3 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 5\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}3 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since ${c}_{22}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are in row echelon form."] [17592186129772 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186129772 :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{21}$ and ${a}_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟶\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form."] [17592186129773 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186129773 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ and $C$ are in row echelon form."] [17592186129774 :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n$A = \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 3\\end{array}\\right], B = \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6\\end{array}\\right], C = \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]$."] [17592186129779 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186129779 :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{22}$, ${a}_{32}$ and ${a}_{33}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟶\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form."] [17592186235821 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186235871 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186235879 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186235912 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186235931 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186236097 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186236128 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186236215 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186236231 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186236337 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186236421 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186236593 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186236920 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186237011 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186237077 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186237177 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}5 & 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186237219 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186237275 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186237357 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186237402 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186237897 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$"] [17592186237897 :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186237988 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form."] [17592186237988 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form using the standard algorithm for Gaussian elimination, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{13}{2} & -\\frac{13}{2}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{13}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186238184 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}6 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 9\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186238272 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 14\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=13$ and $y=14$"] [17592186238432 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186238552 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186238587 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{11}{16}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186238598 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{13}{16}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186238787 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-4$ and $y=1$"] [17592186238806 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186238843 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}6 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186238893 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 13\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=11$ and $y=13$"] [17592186239014 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{15}{16}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186239148 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-8$ and $y=2$"] [17592186239163 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186239165 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186239298 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186239309 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186239668 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{17}{16}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186239691 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -11\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=19$ and $y=-11$"] [17592186239705 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}6 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186239747 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186239803 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -16\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=17$ and $y=-16$"] [17592186239838 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -6\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186240071 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 11\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=10$ and $y=11$"] [17592186240208 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-2$ and $y=2$"] [17592186240213 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-4$ and $y=1$"] [17592186240226 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186240258 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{9}{16}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186240263 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186240305 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186240309 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-2$ and $y=2$"] [17592186240971 :question/problem "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of equations?\n\n$$\\begin{cases}x+3y=7 \\\\ 2x=4\\end{cases}$$"] [17592186240971 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered  $x,y$ \n on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 \\\\ 2x+0y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186241007 :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\\begin{cases}2x-5=3y \\\\ 6+y=4x\\end{cases}$$"] [17592186241007 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\\begin{cases}2x-3y=5 \\\\ -4x+y=-6\\end{cases}$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations: \n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -6\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [17592186241137 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix."] [17592186241137 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-1\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -11\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & x+y=8 \\\\  & y=-11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-11$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(-11)=8\\,⇒\\,x=8+11=19.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=19$ and $y=-11.$"] [17592186241149 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 8\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form."] [17592186241149 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form using the standard algorithm for Gaussian elimination, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 16 & 13\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{13}{16}\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186241392 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -11 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186241465 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186241493 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186241697 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186241708 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & -3 & 1 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0 & -15 \\\\ 20 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186241923 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & -3 & -1 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0 & -15 \\\\ 20 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186242105 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186242182 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186242267 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -11 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186242568 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186242621 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 3 & -1 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0 & -15 \\\\ 20 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186242674 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & -3 & -1 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186242720 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186242781 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -11 & 0 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186242853 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & -3 & -1 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0 & -15 \\\\ 20 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186243890 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y-z=16 \\\\ -8x+4y+0z=-15 \\\\ 20x-y-3z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}9 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -1 & -3\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 3 & -1 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0 & -15 \\\\ 20 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [17592186243994 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}-11{x}_{3}=12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186244042 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $a,b,c$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}a-4b-c=0 \\\\ -11a+0b+3c=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ -11 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [17592186259882 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & \\,\\,\\,-8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 3 & \\,\\,\\,-1 & \\,\\,\\,-8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186259972 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 4 & 2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186260045 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & \\,\\,\\,-8 & 1 & \\,\\,\\,-1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\,\\,\\,-1 & \\,\\,\\,-8 & \\,\\,\\,-4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186260089 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}\\frac{1}{3} & 5 & 11 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 6 & 1 & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186260116 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -9 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186260176 :answer/value "\